  "response": "The physical rationale for the search is straightforward: primordial gravitational waves from cosmic inflation would imprint a curl-like B-mode pattern on the polarization of the cosmic microwave background, which is why degree-scale polarimetry became the decisive observational target of the past decade. The BICEP2 telescope made its observations from the Atacama Desert in Chile.\n\nIn March 2014 the BICEP2 collaboration reported a detection of B-mode polarization at degree angular scales; expressed in terms of the amplitude of tensor perturbations, that is to say, the collaboration reported a tensor-to-scalar ratio of r = 0.20. The announcement was presented as the first observational handle on the energy scale of inflation, and it was scrutinized within weeks by specialists in Galactic emission.\n\nThe BICEP2 instrument observed the microwave sky at a single frequency of 150 gigahertz, whereas polarized emission from Galactic dust produces a B-mode signal that mimics the inflationary signature. Precisely because dust can counterfeit the cosmological pattern in this way, Raphael Flauger and David Spergel argued that the BICEP2 team had underestimated the polarized dust foreground in its observing field. After that critique began to circulate, the Planck collaboration released maps of polarized Galactic dust emission in September 2014. Planck's polarized dust maps for the BICEP2 region were first published in 2016, a chronology that cannot be squared with their appearance in September of 2014. The September 2014 dust release was followed, on 30 January 2015, by the public appearance of the joint BICEP2/Keck Array and Planck analysis.\n\nDust emission and primordial B modes have different brightness spectra across microwave frequencies, and that difference brought about observations with the Keck Array at 95 gigahertz, which supplied a second frequency channel for foreground separation. The measurements from that second frequency channel show that the joint BICEP2/Keck Array and Planck analysis found no statistically significant evidence of primordial B-mode polarization. Either that null outcome is what the joint treatment of the data delivered, or the joint analysis confirmed a statistically significant detection of primordial B-mode polarization; one of the two must be the case, and they cannot both be.\n\nThe episode is therefore a textbook case of adjudication by instrument rather than by argument. Competing readings of a single-band measurement could be weighed against each other indefinitely, since the cosmological and Galactic interpretations predicted the same angular pattern on the sky; what closed the question was the acquisition of data at a second wavelength and the sharing of foreground maps between collaborations, which converted an interpretive dispute into a measurable separation of components. The reported amplitude was accordingly replaced by an upper limit on r, and the search for inflationary B modes continued with multi-frequency instruments designed from the outset to characterize dust.",
